---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const dataCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · CSV — the plain table', note: 'Comma-separated values: one row per line. Boring, universal, opens anywhere. Most of the world\'s data has touched a CSV.' },
    { math: '2 · JSON — labelled objects', note: '{"name": "Asha", "age": 12}. Human-readable, nesting-friendly, the lingua franca of web APIs.' },
    { math: '3 · Tables have a schema', note: 'Decide what columns mean and what types they hold. A schema is a promise about the data — the reason databases can be trusted more than spreadsheets.' },
    { math: '4 · Files live in trees', note: 'Directories within directories, paths like addresses. Every operating system, cloud bucket and repository shares this tree model.' },
  ],
};

const dataQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: 'A CSV row reads: Smith,John,"London, UK",34. Why are there quotes around the third field?',
      choices: [
        'Quotes mark text fields that spellcheckers should skip',
        'The value itself contains a comma — RFC 4180 quoting keeps "London, UK" as one field instead of splitting into "London" and " UK" and shifting every later column',
        'Quotes make the file smaller',
        'It is a typo that will be ignored',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'The comma is both the separator and a legal character inside values, so the format defines quoting (and doubled quotes "") to embed it. Naive parsers that split on every comma corrupt the table silently — one reason "I will just parse it myself" is the most common CSV bug.',
    },
    {
      q: 'You open a text file and see "cafÃ©" where "café" should be. What happened?',
      choices: [
        'The file is damaged on disk',
        'An encoding mismatch: the file is UTF-8 (é stored as two bytes) but was read as Windows-1252/Latin-1, which shows each byte as a separate character — mojibake, the classic symptom',
        'The text was encrypted',
        'The font lacks the letter',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Text files are bytes plus an interpretation. UTF-8 stores é as two bytes; reading them one-per-character produces "Ã©". The fix is to read and write with an agreed encoding — UTF-8 everywhere is the modern default — and the lesson is that "plain text" always has an encoding.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Your program must store a class register: names, ages, whether homework was submitted. In file-handling terms, the cleanest design is:',
      choices: [
        'One file per student, named after the student',
        'A table: one record per student, fields for each attribute — stored as a header row plus rows in CSV, or objects in a JSON array — because records of the same kind belong together with a declared structure',
        'A single long sentence containing everything',
        'Random sticky notes',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Data of the same kind belongs in one structure with a schema: columns (or fields) that mean the same thing in every row. This is what makes the file readable by programs (loops over rows), by other people (headers explain it) and by future-you (the schema is the documentation).',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why do long-lived datasets prefer simple formats like CSV or JSON over proprietary application files?',
      choices: [
        'Simple formats are always smaller',
        'Plain text formats are readable by any tool, decade after decade, with no dependency on a particular application surviving — the "readable in ten years" principle; proprietary binary files die with their software',
        'Regulators forbid binary files',
        'Spreadsheets cannot hold numbers',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Data outlives software. A CSV from 1995 opens instantly today; a proprietary database file from a defunct application may be unrecoverable. Interoperability — anyone, any language, any era — is the reason standards like RFC 4180 and RFC 8259 exist and why archives demand open formats.',
    },
  ],
};

const dfilesFuturePredict = {
  "q": "When you pick the format for a dataset, which reader matters most?",
  "choices": [
    "You, or a colleague, ten years from now — with whatever software exists then",
    "Only the program that wrote it",
    "The operating system's file manager",
    "Whoever owns the most expensive spreadsheet"
  ],
  "answer": 0,
  "why": "A dataset outlives the session that made it; the format is a promise about who can still open it — plain, standard formats keep that promise."
};

const dfilesEncSort = {
  "prompt": "Byte-story facts — sort each under the encoding it describes:",
  "buckets": [
    "ASCII (1963)",
    "Unicode & its byte-encoding"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "mapped 128 values — enough for 1963 America",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "one catalogue for every character in every language, plus emoji",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "English letters, digits and punctuation in single bytes",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "rarer characters spill into two, three or four bytes",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "useless for the rest of the world's writing systems",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "backward-compatible: the old single-byte characters kept their codes",
      "bucket": 1
    }
  ],
  "why": "The modern default kept the old single-byte range unchanged and gave every rarer character room — that is why it won."
};

const dfilesPlainJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "Every text file stores byte numbers; characters appear only when reader and writer agree on a decoding table.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The mojibake symptom is disagreement, not damage."
    },
    {
      "s": "'Plain text' needs no decisions — letters are simply stored as letters.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "There is always an encoding; the only question is whether it was chosen deliberately."
    },
    {
      "s": "Mojibake means the bytes are damaged and need a repair tool.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "The bytes are intact; agree on the encoding and the text reads fine."
    },
    {
      "s": "One encoding now rules almost everywhere because it keeps English text unchanged and covers every writing system.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Backward compatible and complete — one decision, made once."
    }
  ]
};

const dfilesGrainBranch = {
  root: "You are designing the weather dataset from the paragraph — one row per what?",
  nodes: {
    start: {
      text: "You have daily readings for 40 weather stations over ten years: temperature, rainfall, wind.",
      choices: [
        { label: "One row per station — 40 rows, 3,650 columns", next: "wide", feedback: "Every new year means rebuilding the schema; comparing two days means leaping across hundreds of columns." },
        { label: "One row per station per day", next: "grain", feedback: "1,460,000 rows, nine columns — tall, uniform, and every tool from spreadsheets to SQL handles it the same way." },
        { label: "One file per station, format chosen per station", next: "fragile", feedback: "Forty formats means forty parsers; the analysis now starts with an archaeology dig." }
      ]
    },
    wide: {
      text: "The wide layout works until the first schema change.",
      choices: [
        { label: "Try adding hourly readings", next: "wideWorse", feedback: "24 more columns per day — the table now has a shape nobody wants to query." },
        { label: "Restart with one row per station per day", next: "grain", feedback: "The tall layout absorbs new dates, new stations and new fields without touching the schema." }
      ]
    },
    wideWorse: { text: "The table sprawls: one station's year is a row of 8,760 mostly-empty cells. Analysis tools were built for tall tables — and so were you." },
    grain: {
      text: "The grain is set. Now the address field: \"12 Collingwood Rd, Bristol\" in one column.",
      choices: [
        { label: "Keep it as one field — it reads fine", next: "crammed", feedback: "Sorting by city or deduplicating by street now needs string surgery on every row." },
        { label: "Split house, street, city into separate fields", next: "atomic", feedback: "Three boring columns; every future query gets to be a simple comparison." }
      ]
    },
    crammed: { text: "The crammed field works until the first \"group by city\" — which becomes a parse-and-pray operation over 1.4 million rows." },
    atomic: { text: "Atomic fields, one clean grain — the dataset now survives new years, new stations and new questions." },
    fragile: { text: "Per-station formats trade one design decision for forty. Consistency is the cheaper contract." }
  },
  why: "Grain first, atomicity second: one row per what decides everything downstream, and one-value-per-field keeps every future query trivial."
};

const dfilesCsvSpot = {
  "prompt": "One of these claims about the plain-table format is backwards — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "CSV is plain text: openable in any editor, spreadsheet or language, with no software dependency.",
    "CSV has no opinion on types — each reader re-decides what \"34\" is.",
    "Spreadsheets are careful with guesses: leading zeros on barcodes are always preserved.",
    "Some countries export semicolon-CSV because the comma is their decimal point.",
    "Line endings differ between Windows and Unix.",
    "So a CSV travels with a schema note: which separator, which encoding, what each attribute means and holds."
  ],
  "errorLine": 2,
  "correction": "The guesses mangle: leading zeros vanish from barcodes, long account numbers become floating point, \"3/4\" turns into a date.",
  "why": "Type-blindness is the price of universality — the schema note is the apology that travels with it."
};

const dfilesCsvJsonCompare = {
  "prompt": "One table, two philosophies — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "CSV",
    "text": "positional columns in plain text — universal, type-blind, simplest when the table is wide and flat"
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "JSON",
    "text": "labels travel with values and objects nest in arrays — the lingua franca of web APIs"
  },
  "why": "Wide and flat → positions; nested and labelled → names. Mind the edges: dates are text, huge integers lose precision, trailing commas are illegal."
};

const dfilesSchemaMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each promise with the clause that keeps it:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "what an empty cell means",
      "right": "unknown, not-applicable or zero — declared, never guessed"
    },
    {
      "left": "what units",
      "right": "metres or centimetres — the 1999 orbiter was lost mixing pound-seconds with newton-seconds"
    },
    {
      "left": "what range is legal",
      "right": "score is 0–100 or missing"
    },
    {
      "left": "what type each attribute holds",
      "right": "student_id a unique integer; name text; submitted true or false"
    }
  ],
  "why": "A promise about meaning and types — the ten-year test judges whether it survives."
};

const dfilesReadOrder = {
  "prompt": "The canonical read-a-table pattern, in order:",
  "items": [
    "Open for reading — name the path and the mode",
    "Use or skip the header row",
    "Loop over the rows, splitting each into fields",
    "Convert types and accumulate — totals, counts, bests",
    "Close the handle — buffered data dies if you never do"
  ],
  "why": "Open, read, close — plus the robustness habits: the blank last row, the quoted comma, the value that should be a number but is 'N/A'."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/intermediate/data-files-tables-json">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Computer Science lesson. You need the foundational lesson
    <em>Binary and Bits</em> — bytes are about to become characters — and the intermediate
    <em>Programming Fundamentals</em>, because variables, loops and arrays are the tools
    that manipulate data in memory before and after it crosses to disk. Nothing beyond
    those is assumed; every format is explained from its first principles here.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why "plain text" is always bytes plus an encoding, and diagnose mojibake;</li>
    <li>design a table with a sensible grain and atomic fields, and write the schema note that travels with it;</li>
    <li>read and write CSV correctly, including quoted commas, dialects and missing values;</li>
    <li>read and write JSON objects and arrays, and choose between CSV and JSON for a given job;</li>
    <li>apply versioning, provenance and backup habits so a dataset survives its own pipeline.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Before algorithms and databases, a practical question: <strong>how is data written down, and
    how will it be read back?</strong> Programs end; files persist — which makes file handling
    the part of programming where the future is a design decision. You already know the
    programming concepts (variables, loops, arrays) that manipulate data in memory; this
    page is about the boundary: how records cross from memory to disk and back, in formats
    each with a philosophy — plain tables (CSV), labelled objects (JSON), tagged documents
    (XML/HTML), binary efficiency (Parquet, images). Choosing a format is choosing who can
    read your data ten years from now. This page covers files and the file tree, text and
    encoding, the record-and-field table model, file handling operations as programmers
    actually use them, the two workhorse text formats in depth, schemas as promises, and
    the quality habits that keep data usable.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="bet before you read on — who is the format for?" widget="predict" config={dfilesFuturePredict} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four facts that make data files usable" widget="equationStep" config={dataCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four cards in order — CSV, JSON,
    schema, file tree — and treat each as one fact you will use for the rest of the
    lesson. The first two are formats, the third is the promise that makes either format
    trustworthy, and the fourth is where the files live. Together they are the minimum
    kit for getting data back out of a file unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2>Files, bytes and encoding: text is an interpretation</h2>
  <p>
    At the hardware level, a file is just a sequence of bytes — numbers from 0 to 255.
    What makes it a <em>text</em> file is an <strong>encoding</strong>: the agreed table that maps
    byte patterns to characters. ASCII, the 1963 classic, mapped 128 values to English
    letters, digits and punctuation — fine for 1963 America, useless for the rest of the
    world's writing systems. The solution was <strong>Unicode</strong> (one catalogue for every
    character in every language, plus emoji) and <strong>UTF-8</strong>, its clever byte-encoding:
    ASCII characters use one identical byte (backward compatibility), while anything
    rarer uses two, three or four bytes. UTF-8 is now the default almost everywhere,
    and its failure mode is instructive: read a UTF-8 file with the older Latin-1
    interpretation and "café" appears as "cafÃ©" — <em>mojibake</em>, the visible
    fingerprint of two encodings disagreeing. The lesson carries beyond text: every
    file format is bytes plus an interpretation, and corrupt output usually means the
    writer and reader disagreed about the interpretation — encoding, byte order or
    structure — rather than the file being physically damaged.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="file each fact under the encoding it describes" widget="sort-buckets" config={dfilesEncSort} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a text file is just letters stored as letters — there
  is nothing to decide." Every text file stores byte numbers, and the characters only
  appear because reader and writer agree on the same decoding table. The mojibake symptom
  is not damage but disagreement, which is why the fix is always an encoding decision
  (UTF-8 everywhere is the modern default), never a repair tool.</p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — what 'plain text' really is" widget="judge" config={dfilesPlainJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a file is bytes plus an interpretation, and the file tree
    gives every file a path — an address, not a shelf. Next: the shape of the data inside
    the file, which is the same everywhere — records and fields.
  </p>

  <h2>The file tree: where data lives</h2>
  <p>
    Every operating system, cloud bucket and code repository organises files the same
    way: a <strong>tree</strong>. Directories (folders) contain files and other directories,
    recursively; the location of a file is its <strong>path</strong> — the sequence of names
    from the root ("addresses" like <code>documents/school/science/results.csv</code>).
    The tree model matters to programmers for practical reasons: paths are how programs
    find data; <strong>extensions</strong> (.csv, .json, .txt) are conventions telling humans
    and software what interpretation to expect (changing the extension does not change
    the bytes, which is why renaming a broken file never fixed it); and file
    <strong>metadata</strong> — size, timestamps, permissions — lives in the directory
    structure, not the file. Good data projects extend the tree logic into
    <strong>naming discipline</strong>: consistent, meaningful, dated names
    (<code>results_2026-10-31.csv</code>) are the cheapest form of documentation,
    because a folder listing is a table of contents nobody had to write.
  </p>

  <h2>Records, fields and the table model</h2>
  <p>
    Most useful data is <strong>many things of the same kind</strong> — students, transactions,
    measurements — and the universal way to structure it is the table: each <strong>row</strong>
    (record) is one thing; each <strong>column</strong> (field) is one attribute, holding the
    same kind of value in every row. In memory, a program holds this as an array of
    records (or objects); on disk, the two great text formats express it differently.
    The design skill is deciding the <strong>grain</strong> — one row per what? A weather
    dataset might be one row per station per day; choosing "one row per station,
    365 columns" would be technically possible and practically unusable. Atomic
    values are the other discipline: each field holds one value of one type —
    "address" crammed into one field defeats sorting and searching; splitting house,
    street, city into separate fields costs nothing now and pays forever.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="design the dataset — grain and atomicity as decisions" widget="branch" config={dfilesGrainBranch} />

  <h2>CSV: the plain table, with traps</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>CSV</strong> (comma-separated values) is the plainest possible table: one row per
    line, fields separated by commas, usually a header row naming the columns. Its
    virtues are its plainness — openable in any text editor, spreadsheet, database
    tool or programming language, with no software dependency — which is why most
    of the world's data has touched a CSV at some point (formalised in RFC 4180).
    Its traps are equally plain. <strong>The comma inside a value:</strong> a row like
    <code>Smith,John,"London, UK",34</code> needs quoting rules (values containing
    commas are wrapped in quotes; literal quotes are doubled) — and a parser that
    ignores the rules splits "London" and " UK" into separate fields, silently
    shifting every column after it. <strong>No types:</strong> CSV has no opinion on
    whether "34" is a number, a code or a post-town; every reader re-decides, and
    spreadsheets famously mangle things they guess about (leading zeros vanish from
    barcodes, long account numbers become floating point, "3/4" becomes a date).
    <strong>No standard for dialects:</strong> separators vary by locale (some countries
    export semicolon-CSV because the comma is their decimal point), and line endings
    differ between Windows and Unix. None of this kills CSV — it means CSV files
    travel with a <strong>schema note</strong>: which separator, which encoding (UTF-8),
    what each column means and holds.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="find the faulty line — the plain table's traps" widget="spot-error" config={dfilesCsvSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> CSV writes a table as plain text — universal, but blind to
    types and full of quoting traps, so it travels with a schema note. The next format
    replaces positional columns with labels.
  </p>

  <h2>JSON: labelled objects for nested data</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>JSON</strong> (JavaScript Object Notation, specified in RFC 8259) writes data as
    labelled structures instead of positions. An object is braces around
    "key": value pairs; arrays are brackets around ordered values; and the two nest
    freely — an object can hold arrays, arrays can hold objects, which is how one
    JSON document expresses a whole school: each student an object with an array of
    subject scores, each subject an object with its own fields. This
    <strong>self-describing quality</strong> (labels travel with values) and the easy
    nesting made JSON the lingua franca of web APIs: a weather service returns an
    object whose fields include a nested forecast array, and every language has a
    parser built in. Its edges are worth knowing the way you know a tool's edges:
    <strong>no date type</strong> (dates become strings, so pick an unambiguous format like
    ISO 8601 — "2026-10-31"); <strong>no comments</strong>; <strong>numbers lose precision</strong>
    for very large integers (JavaScript's number type tops out near 9 × 10¹⁵, so
    bank-account or product IDs are exchanged as strings); and <strong>strictness</strong> —
    trailing commas and single quotes are illegal, so hand-editing JSON breaks
    easily. For wide flat tables, CSV stays simpler; for nested, hierarchical,
    labelled data, JSON wins outright.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="two formats, two philosophies — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={dfilesCsvJsonCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "JSON belongs to JavaScript." The name is history; the
  format itself is a language-independent standard (RFC 8259) with parsers built into
  Python, Java, Rust, spreadsheets and everything else that trades data on the web. JSON
  documents are read and written by programs that have never heard of a browser.</p>

  <h2>Schemas: the promise about the data</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>schema</strong> is the declaration of what the columns (or fields) mean and
    what types they hold: <em>student_id is a unique integer; name is text; submitted
    is true or false; score is 0–100 or missing</em>. The schema is a promise, and it
    is what separates a table a program can trust from a grid a human squints at.
    It answers the questions every future reader has: what does an empty cell mean
    (unknown, not-applicable, zero?); what units (metres, centimetres — the 1999
    Mars Climate Orbiter was lost because two teams mixed pound-seconds and
    newton-seconds); what range is legal. Schemas live in various bodies: a README,
    a formal standard (JSON Schema), a database's type system, or the header row
    plus conventions. The <strong>ten-year test</strong> is the useful one: will a colleague
    with no memory of this project, opening the file in 2036, be able to use it
    correctly? Plain formats, a written schema, atomic fields, consistent types and
    one row per thing get to yes.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each promise with the clause that keeps it" widget="match" config={dfilesSchemaMatch} />

  <h2>File handling: the operations</h2>
  <p>
    In code, working with files follows the same rhythm in every language:
    <strong>open</strong> (name the path and the mode — read, write, append; write mode
    typically replaces the file's contents, which is how data gets accidentally
    destroyed, and append adds to the end), <strong>read or write</strong> (whole file at
    once, or line by line for big files — reading a 2 GB log one line at a time
    keeps memory flat where loading it whole does not), and <strong>close</strong> (release
    the handle; unclosed files can lose buffered data). Pseudocode syllabus
    versions (OPENFILE, READFILE, WRITEFILE, CLOSEFILE) compress the same ideas.
    The two canonical patterns: <strong>append a record</strong> (open in append mode,
    write one formatted line, close — the log-file pattern), and
    <strong>process a table</strong> (open for reading, skip or use the header, loop over
    lines, split each into fields, convert types, accumulate). A worked fragment
    in spirit: for each line in the file, split on commas, keep fields 1 and 4,
    total the numbers — and the robustness habits appear the moment real data
    arrives: handle the blank last line, handle the quoted comma, handle the
    value that should be a number but is "N/A".
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="processing a table from disk — the canonical pattern" widget="order" config={dfilesReadOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a spreadsheet is a data file. A spreadsheet is an
  application's document — formatting, formulas, merged cells, multiple sheets —
  of which data is only part, and which machines parse badly. The professional
  habit is to keep the <em>data</em> in plain tabular files (CSV/JSON) and treat
  spreadsheets as one tool that can view them, not as the storage format
  itself.</p>

  <h2>The file's lifecycle: versioning and provenance</h2>
  <p>
    Files that matter outlive the session that made them, so professional data
    work adds two habits beyond the format question. <strong>Versioning:</strong>
    never overwrite the only copy of anything — keep the raw download
    immutable (the <code>raw/</code> directory that nothing edits), write
    processed results to a new file or a dated version
    (<code>results_2026-10-31.csv</code>), and let version-control systems
    (git) track text data where size allows. The reason is empirical:
    every cleaning pipeline has bugs, and the ability to re-run from raw is
    the difference between a recoverable mistake and a destroyed dataset.
    <strong>Provenance:</strong> record where each file came from — the source
    URL or system, the date, the tool and its version — in a small README
    beside the data. The ten-year test from the schema section extends
    naturally: a future reader (probably you) needs to know not just what
    the columns mean but which download and which cleaning script produced
    them. Backup completes the lifecycle by the 3-2-1 rule of thumb —
    three copies, two kinds of storage, one off-site — because "the file
    was on the laptop" is the most common sentence in data-loss
    stories. Formats decide whether data can be read; the
    lifecycle decides whether it still exists.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A CSV row reads: Smith,John,"London, UK",34. Why are there quotes around the third field?</li>
    <li>You open a file and see "cafÃ©" where "café" should be. Is the file damaged?</li>
    <li>Your program must store a class register — names, ages, homework submitted. What structure do you choose, and why?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The value itself contains a comma, so RFC 4180 quoting
    keeps "London, UK" as one field instead of splitting it and shifting every later
    column. (2) No — that is mojibake: the bytes are UTF-8 but were read with a
    one-byte-per-character interpretation; agree on UTF-8 and the text is intact. (3) A
    table: one record per student, one field per attribute, stored as header-plus-rows in
    CSV or objects in a JSON array — records of the same kind belong together under a
    declared schema.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every file is bytes plus an interpretation; corrupt output usually means writer and reader disagreed.</li>
    <li>The file tree gives data addresses; extensions and naming discipline are documentation for free.</li>
    <li>A table is rows of records and columns of fields; the grain — one row per what — is the first design decision.</li>
    <li>CSV is universal but type-blind and trap-prone; it travels with a schema note.</li>
    <li>JSON labels values and nests freely — the lingua franca of web APIs — but has no date type and strict syntax.</li>
    <li>A schema is a promise about meaning and types; the ten-year test judges whether the promise survives.</li>
    <li>Keep raw data immutable, record provenance, and follow the 3-2-1 backup rule.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling lesson <em>Algorithms: Searching &amp; Sorting</em> shows what programs do
    with well-formed tables once they are in memory, and <em>Logic Gates &amp; Digital
    Circuits</em> drops beneath the bytes to the hardware that stores them. The
    data-science ladder then takes over: cleaning, querying and charting the very files
    this lesson teaches you to write.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Binary and columnar formats.</strong> Text formats trade efficiency for
    universality; at scale, the trade flips. Binary formats like Parquet store
    data <em>column by column</em> with compression, so a query reading one column
    of a billion rows skips the rest entirely — which is why the data-engineering
    world moved from CSV to Parquet for anything large, while keeping CSV/JSON
    as the interchange layer. HDF5 serves the same role for scientific arrays.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Files versus databases.</strong> Once multiple users write simultaneously,
    integrity questions (two programs editing one file; half-written records
    after a crash) exceed what files answer. Databases add transactions,
    indexing and query languages — the next rung of the ladder. The design
    skill transfers unchanged, though: the table, the schema and the atomic
    field are the same ideas in both worlds, which is why this lesson's
    disciplines are the foundation the database lesson builds on.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Data cleaning starts at writing.</strong> The data-science ladder
    dedicates a lesson to cleaning because real data arrives dirty — missing
    values, duplicates, three date formats in one column. But every cleaning
    problem is cheaper to prevent at write time: headers on every file, one
    type per column, ISO dates, an explicit missing-value convention, and
    raw data kept immutably beside the cleaned version. The cheapest
    cleaning pass is the one that never runs.
  </p>
